Subject: [PATCH 2/5] offset_check.py: 16-byte steps, rescans instead of a
 threshold, eight pairs a confirmation; thread stacks only

On the i7-12700K the effect is +9-11% in a window 48 bytes wide, and the
check could not see it (exit 3 at steps of 128 and 64). Three causes,
each fixed here:

- Steps of 64 jump over a 48-byte window: the scan now steps 16 bytes,
  the stack's alignment (--step replaces --coarse).
- A 10% screen sits above a 9% effect: each cell's three worst offsets
  are rescanned twice and ranked by the lowest of their three readings,
  which one stray reading cannot raise; regress alone decides.
- Four pairs cannot clear Benjamini-Hochberg over 16 cells: the slow
  offset ran +8% to +20% in every one of four pairs and was judged
  level. A confirmation now runs eight.

The main thread's stack is left out: its top holds the command line,
which regress's runs do not share with the scan, so a main-stack offset
found in the scan lands elsewhere when confirmed (one read 1.38 times
the median in three scans and level under regress).

On the i7, 5 of 8 runs now exit 0 (servil 2e892e1: 3 of 5; 082afa3: 2 of
3); in the other three the scan's candidates missed the window.
